 "world_update.resource": "The cluster's compute capacity is largely partitioned into stable, dedicated slices for each service, with a modest shared pool reserved for spillover. These baseline slices do not automatically re-balance across services within decision rounds, so most of the capacity remains tied to specific services and only a smaller fraction can be flexibly assigned via the shared pool. In off-peak periods, some services operate comfortably within their slices, but others already run relatively close to their reserved limits. During predictable peak intervals, aggregate requested compute across all services frequently reaches, and occasionally slightly exceeds, the physical capacity of the cluster. In roughly half of the rounds, at least one service needs more compute than its slice plus a reasonable draw from the shared pool can provide without edging the cluster into overload. When total demand nears capacity, satisfying rising demand for one service tends to reduce what can be feasibly provided to at least one other service, because the shared buffer is not large enough to absorb all excess. Some services in these rounds will see part of their requested capacity unmet, with observable performance degradation, while others can be fully served. However, there remain rounds, especially during lower demand phases, where all services can be accommodated simultaneously. This regime requires clear trade-offs among services in a significant fraction of rounds but does not yet entail that hard trade-offs are unavoidable in almost every decision, which distinguishes it from more acute shortage conditions.",
